WEBVTT

00:00.590 --> 00:05.360
Let's say we have the
repeating decimal 0.4008,

00:05.360 --> 00:08.475
where the digits 4008
keep on repeating.

00:08.475 --> 00:09.850
So if we were to
write it out, it

00:09.850 --> 00:11.141
would look something like this.

00:11.141 --> 00:19.790
0.400840084008, and it
keeps on going forever.

00:19.790 --> 00:22.070
What I want you to do right
now is pause the video

00:22.070 --> 00:23.778
and think about whether
you can represent

00:23.778 --> 00:26.250
this repeating decimal
as an infinite sum,

00:26.250 --> 00:27.880
as an infinite series.

00:27.880 --> 00:30.770
And then think about
whether that infinite series

00:30.770 --> 00:33.554
is a geometric series.

00:33.554 --> 00:35.220
So I'm assuming you've
given a go at it.

00:35.220 --> 00:36.840
So let's think about it.

00:36.840 --> 00:39.500
So for each term of
my infinite series,

00:39.500 --> 00:42.310
I'm going to represent one
of these repeating patterns

00:42.310 --> 00:44.210
of 4008.

00:44.210 --> 00:50.570
So, for example, I will make
this 4008 my first term.

00:50.570 --> 00:52.460
So this could be viewed as 0.

00:52.460 --> 00:58.060
And this 4008 represents 0.4008.

00:58.060 --> 01:01.860
Then I could make this
4008 my next term,

01:01.860 --> 01:06.760
or my next term will
represent this 4008.

01:06.760 --> 01:15.130
And this 4008 is the
same thing as 0.00004008.

01:15.130 --> 01:19.140
And then this next
4008, well, that

01:19.140 --> 01:22.200
represents 0 point-- and
we have eight zeroes--

01:22.200 --> 01:30.380
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6,
7, 8, 4008, 4008.

01:30.380 --> 01:33.259
And then we would just keep
on going like that forever.

01:33.259 --> 01:35.550
So we're just going to keep
on going like that forever.

01:35.550 --> 01:38.490
So hopefully there's
a pattern here.

01:38.490 --> 01:41.560
We're essentially throwing
four zeroes before the decimal

01:41.560 --> 01:42.880
every time.

01:42.880 --> 01:45.026
And we can just keep on
going like that forever.

01:45.026 --> 01:46.150
So this is an infinite sum.

01:46.150 --> 01:47.750
It's an infinite series.

01:47.750 --> 01:51.770
The next question is, is
this a geometric series?

01:51.770 --> 01:53.750
Well, in order for it to
be a geometric series,

01:53.750 --> 01:55.290
to go from one term
to the next, you

01:55.290 --> 01:57.450
must be multiplying
by the same value,

01:57.450 --> 01:59.810
by the same common ratio.

01:59.810 --> 02:04.420
So what are we multiplying when
we go from 0.4008 to this one

02:04.420 --> 02:08.120
right over here, where we add
four zeros before the 4008?

02:08.120 --> 02:09.960
What are we multiplying?

02:09.960 --> 02:12.490
Well, we moved the decimal
four spots to the left.

02:12.490 --> 02:15.230
So we're multiplying by
10 to the negative fourth.

02:15.230 --> 02:16.980
Or you could view it
as we're multiplying

02:16.980 --> 02:24.581
by 0.0001, 10 to the
negative 1, 2, 3, 4.

02:24.581 --> 02:26.330
To go from here to
here, well, same thing.

02:26.330 --> 02:28.820
Move the decimal four
places to the left.

02:28.820 --> 02:34.331
So once again, we're
multiplying by 0.0001.

02:34.331 --> 02:35.830
And so it looks
pretty clear that we

02:35.830 --> 02:39.540
have a common ratio of 10 to
the negative fourth power.

02:39.540 --> 02:42.750
So we can rewrite
all of this business

02:42.750 --> 02:51.180
as 0.4008 times our common
ratio for this first term

02:51.180 --> 02:54.980
times our common ratio of
10 to the negative fourth

02:54.980 --> 02:57.310
to the 0-th power--
so that gives us

02:57.310 --> 03:05.040
that right over there--
plus 0.4008 times 10

03:05.040 --> 03:07.840
to the negative fourth
to the first power.

03:07.840 --> 03:10.560
And that gives us that
value right over there.

03:10.560 --> 03:19.900
Plus 0.4008 times 10 to the
negative 4 to the second power.

03:19.900 --> 03:20.885
And we keep on going.

03:20.885 --> 03:23.010
And so in this form, it
looks a little bit clearer,

03:23.010 --> 03:26.400
like a geometric series, an
infinite geometric series.

03:26.400 --> 03:29.150
And if we wanted to write
that out with sigma notation,

03:29.150 --> 03:33.510
we could write this
as the sum from k

03:33.510 --> 03:37.825
equals 0 to infinity
of, well, what's

03:37.825 --> 03:38.950
our first term going to be?

03:38.950 --> 03:49.981
It's going to be 0.4008
times our common ratio, which

03:49.981 --> 03:52.230
we could write out as either
10 to the negative fourth

03:52.230 --> 03:54.290
or 0.0001.

03:54.290 --> 03:56.450
I'll just write it as 10
to the negative fourth.

03:56.450 --> 04:02.120
10 to the negative fourth to the
k-th power, to the k-th power.

04:02.120 --> 04:03.910
So the next
interesting question--

04:03.910 --> 04:07.980
this clearly can be represented
as a geometric series--

04:07.980 --> 04:09.064
is, well, what is the sum?

04:09.064 --> 04:10.646
You might say, well,
that's just going

04:10.646 --> 04:12.280
to be 4008 repeating
over and over.

04:12.280 --> 04:14.220
But I want to express
it as a fraction.

04:14.220 --> 04:15.936
And so I want you
to pause the video.

04:15.936 --> 04:17.810
Use what would you
already know about finding

04:17.810 --> 04:20.269
the sum of an infinite
geometric series

04:20.269 --> 04:24.390
to try to express this thing
right over here as a fraction.

04:24.390 --> 04:26.960
So I'm assuming
you've had a go at it.

04:26.960 --> 04:28.480
So let's think about it.

04:28.480 --> 04:29.930
We've already
seen, we've already

04:29.930 --> 04:32.140
derived in previous
videos, that the sum

04:32.140 --> 04:34.240
of an infinite
geometric series--

04:34.240 --> 04:35.880
let me do this in
a neutral color.

04:35.880 --> 04:41.620
If I have a series like this,
k equals 0 to infinity of ar

04:41.620 --> 04:47.500
to the k power, that this sum
is going to be equal to a over 1

04:47.500 --> 04:48.720
minus r.

04:48.720 --> 04:51.360
We've derived this actually
in several other videos.

04:51.360 --> 04:54.040
So in this case, this
is going to be-- well,

04:54.040 --> 04:59.480
our a here is 0.4008.

04:59.480 --> 05:06.590
And it's going to be that over 1
minus our common ratio, minus--

05:06.590 --> 05:14.500
and I'll write it like this--
0.0001, 1 ten thousandth.

05:14.500 --> 05:16.040
So what's this going to be?

05:16.040 --> 05:21.240
Well, this is going to be
the same thing as 0.4008.

05:21.240 --> 05:24.250
If you take 1 minus
1 ten thousandths,

05:24.250 --> 05:27.545
or you could do this as
10,000 ten thousandths minus 1

05:27.545 --> 05:42.177
ten thousandth, you're going
to have 9,999 ten thousandths.

05:42.177 --> 05:44.260
Once again, you could
view-- let me write this out

05:44.260 --> 05:45.790
just so this doesn't
look confusing.

05:45.790 --> 05:50.770
1 is the same thing
as 10,000/10,000.

05:50.770 --> 05:55.150
And you're subtracting 1/10,000.

05:55.150 --> 06:00.520
And so you're going
to get 9,999/10,000.

06:00.520 --> 06:03.760
And so this is going
to be the same thing

06:03.760 --> 06:09.690
as 0.4008 times 10,000.

06:09.690 --> 06:17.880
So times 10,000 over 9,999.

06:17.880 --> 06:20.460
Well, what's this top
number times 10,000?

06:20.460 --> 06:23.870
Well that's just going
to give us 4,008.

06:23.870 --> 06:31.500
4,008 over 9,999.

06:31.500 --> 06:33.600
And we've just expressed
that repeating decimal

06:33.600 --> 06:34.860
as a fraction.

06:34.860 --> 06:35.740
So we have succeeded.

06:35.740 --> 06:38.400
And you might say, well, maybe
we can simplify this thing.

06:38.400 --> 06:39.907
And so let's see.

06:39.907 --> 06:41.740
This is already a
fraction, so we've already

06:41.740 --> 06:42.640
kind of achieved it.

06:42.640 --> 06:44.500
But if we want to get
a little bit simpler.

06:44.500 --> 06:46.930
Let's see, if we add the
digits up here, 4 plus 8 is 12

06:46.930 --> 06:48.170
and 1 plus 2 is 3.

06:48.170 --> 06:50.360
So this up here
is divisible by 3.

06:50.360 --> 06:53.150
And this down here is
clearly divisible by 3.

06:53.150 --> 06:55.090
So let's divide
both of them by 3.

06:55.090 --> 06:58.860
So 3 goes into
4,008-- let's see.

06:58.860 --> 07:03.780
It goes into 4 one time,
subtract, you get a 10.

07:03.780 --> 07:06.000
3 times 3 is 9.

07:06.000 --> 07:08.720
Subtract, you get another 10.

07:08.720 --> 07:09.980
Goes into 3 times.

07:09.980 --> 07:11.480
3 times 3 is 9.

07:11.480 --> 07:12.550
Subtract.

07:12.550 --> 07:13.740
Bring down an 8.

07:13.740 --> 07:16.550
3 into 18 exactly six times.

07:16.550 --> 07:20.760
So our numerator is 1,336.

07:20.760 --> 07:22.770
This is no longer
divisible by 3.

07:22.770 --> 07:25.270
The sum of the digits
is not divisible by 3,

07:25.270 --> 07:27.090
it's not a multiple of 3.

07:27.090 --> 07:34.760
And if you divide this bottom
number by 3, you get 3,333.

07:34.760 --> 07:37.180
And I think we
have simplified it.

07:37.180 --> 07:41.150
I think we can simplified
it about as well as we can.

07:41.150 --> 07:42.470
Well, we could check more.

07:42.470 --> 07:43.600
Let me know if I didn't.

07:43.600 --> 07:46.290
But either way, we
have now written this.

07:46.290 --> 07:47.240
This was pretty neat.

07:47.240 --> 07:48.924
We saw that a
repeating decimal can

07:48.924 --> 07:50.840
be represented not just
as an infinite series,

07:50.840 --> 07:53.040
but as an infinite
geometric series.

07:53.040 --> 07:55.440
And then we were able to use
the formula that we derived

07:55.440 --> 07:57.650
for the sum of an
infinite geometric series

07:57.650 --> 08:00.840
to actually express
it as a fraction.
